-- allium: 3
-- host.allium
--
-- Scope: WHO THIS INSTALL IS — both answers. The machine's own identity: how
--        it comes into existence, what it is allowed to be derived from, and
--        how its display name is changed. And the person's: how an install
--        learns which human it belongs to, and what it does when that answer
--        changes underneath it.
-- Includes: WHERE this install keeps who it is — the host file in the data
--           directory (HostFile, IdentityLivesInHostFile); minting the single
--           local Host row on first run (a board launch with no host
--           file); the one rule that
--           changes the machine's label, and the validation it applies;
--           adopting a UserIdentity offered by a shared store, confirming an
--           unchanged one, and refusing a changed one.
-- Excludes: the Host entity's own shape and the ExactlyOneLocalHost invariant
--           (see core.allium); Task.host, the HostTracksWorktree invariant and
--           the is_locally_owned derivation (also core.allium); the locality gates
--           that consume them (see dispatch.allium: DispatchTask,
--           DispatchResearchTask, ResumeTask, RetryResume, RetryFresh); the
--           worktree-release clears (see tasks.allium: DeleteTask). Also
--           excluded: the connection that offers a UserIdentity in the first
--           place, what subscribing means, and what the board does while the
--           store is unreachable — all of that is sync.allium, which supplies
--           the UserIdentityOffered trigger the rules here answer and consumes
--           the UserIdentitySettled and UserIdentityConflict triggers they
--           emit. Also excluded on the same grounds: getting this machine's
--           OWN Host row TO the shared registry once one exists — RenameHost
--           below emits HostRenamed() and MintHostIdentity's row is read by
--           sync.allium on every connection, but which of those reach a store
--           and when is a connection question, so the sending is
--           sync.allium's (RegisterHostOnConnect, RegisterHostOnRename). Also
--           excluded: WHEN the operator is asked to name this machine and what
--           happens if nobody can answer — that is the startup gate
--           (startup.allium: PromptForHostLabelWhenUnnamed and the rules around
--           it), which asks the question and then hands the answer to
--           RenameHost below. This spec owns what a label may be and what
--           writing one does; startup.allium owns who is asked and when.
--
-- Why this exists as its own file: core.allium's scope excludes rules, and
-- dispatch.allium's covers dispatching an agent, so neither is the right home
-- for a rule that fires once at installation and never again. The pattern
-- this follows is the one core.allium already uses for Worktree and
-- TmuxWindow: the entity is shared domain model and lives there, while the
-- rules that create and act on it live in the spec that owns that behaviour.
--
-- NO RULE HERE READS OR WRITES ANOTHER MACHINE'S ROW, and that is the line to
-- keep. Hosts is a registry now — a shared store puts your colleague's laptop
-- and your own desktop in it — but every rule below resolves the LOCAL row and
-- acts on that. What this file guarantees is that this machine has a stable
-- name for itself, and knows whose it is, before anything is built that needs
-- to compare two.
--
-- THE TWO IDENTITIES ARE NOT ONE, AND THEIR LIFETIMES ARE THE PROOF. The host
-- id is minted offline, on first run, by this install alone, and is never
-- shown to anybody. The user identity is minted by the shared store, on first
-- connection, and is the same value on every machine that person uses. An
-- install can have the first and not the second — permanently, if it never
-- reaches a store — and that is a supported way to run dispatch rather than a
-- half-configured one. Collapsing them would mean either a machine id that
-- changes when somebody buys a laptop, or a person id that cannot exist
-- offline.

use "./core.allium" as core

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities and Variants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The file this install keeps who it is in: `host.json` in the data
-- directory (storage.allium: DataDirectory), beside --db.
--
-- It holds the LOCAL Host row's three fields — core/Host's id, label and
-- owner — and the credential that proves that owner to the store. Nothing
-- else: every setting lives in the store (settings.allium:
-- SettingsAreStoredPerHost), and a key the file does not know is not a
-- setting it should start holding.
entity HostFile {
    path: String               -- <data directory>/config.host_file_name
    permissions: String        -- the file's mode; always config.host_file_mode
    credential: String?        -- the user identity's login credential; null until the first connection hands one out

    -- The file holds a login credential, so only its owner may read it.
    invariant HostFileIsPrivate {
        permissions = config.host_file_mode
    }

    @invariant IdentityLivesInHostFile
        -- This install's identity — host id, host label, user identity and its
        -- credential — is read from and written to the host file and nowhere
        -- else. Not the store: the store holds a MIRROR of the host row
        -- (sync.allium: RegisterHostOnConnect) and never the credential, which
        -- is what the install presents to the store and so cannot live in it.
        -- Not SQLite: that is where these four used to sit, as rows of the old
        -- settings table, and since task #16755 nothing reads them there
        -- (storage.allium: CodeNeverTouchesLegacyDatabase). Nothing is carried
        -- over from it either: an install upgrading past #16755 has no host
        -- file, so its first launch is a first run and mints a fresh identity
        -- (MintHostIdentity).
        --
        -- The host file is what core/local_host() resolves. Every reader and
        -- every writer of the local Host row in this file — MintHostIdentity,
        -- RenameHost, AdoptUserIdentity — is a read or write of it; a one-shot
        -- CLI command reads it and never writes it (cli.allium:
        -- CliCommandsNeedAHostFile).

    @invariant HostFileIsWrittenWhole
        -- Every write replaces the whole file atomically: the new content is
        -- written to a temporary file in the same directory, with the private
        -- mode already applied, and renamed over the old one. A reader
        -- therefore sees the old file or the new one, never half of either;
        -- a crash mid-write leaves the old identity intact rather than a
        -- damaged file. The private mode is set before the content is
        -- written, never afterwards, so the credential is never readable by
        -- anyone else for any length of time.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    -- The host file's name in the data directory.
    host_file_name: String = "host.json"

    -- Owner read/write only: the file carries the login credential.
    host_file_mode: String = "0600"
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules
------------------------------------------------------------

-- == Installation ==

rule MintHostIdentity {
    when: FirstRun()

    requires: core/Hosts.count = 0
    requires: not host_file_present()

    ensures:
        core/Host.created(
            id: generated_opaque_id(),
            label: null
        )
        HostFile.created(
            path: host_file_path(),
            permissions: config.host_file_mode,
            credential: null
        )

    @guidance
        -- A BOARD LAUNCH WITH NO HOST FILE IS A FIRST RUN. A fresh id is
        -- minted, the label is left null, and host.json is written (with the
        -- private mode, as a whole-file replacement) before anything reads
        -- the host. That includes an install upgrading past task #16755: its
        -- old identity sat in the legacy database, which nothing reads
        -- (storage.allium: CodeNeverTouchesLegacyDatabase), so it is a first
        -- run like any other.
        --
        -- A HOST FILE THAT EXISTS BUT CANNOT BE READ MINTS NOTHING. The
        -- `not host_file_present()` guard is what keeps a damaged or
        -- unreadable host.json from being overwritten with a new identity:
        -- no local Host row results, and the startup gate aborts with
        -- host_identity_unavailable (startup.allium:
        -- AbortWhenTheHostIdentityStoreIsUnusable). So does a host file that
        -- cannot be written here.
        --
        -- WHO FIRES FirstRun. The board launch, as its identity step: after
        -- the configuration check and before the host-label gate reads
        -- core/local_host() (startup.allium: CheckHostLabel). A one-shot CLI
        -- command never fires it — with no host file it fails instead
        -- (cli.allium: CliCommandsNeedAHostFile).
        --
        -- Fires once, at installation, before any rule can read a Host. This
        -- is what makes core/Host's ExactlyOneLocalHost invariant safe to
        -- state without an "or zero" arm, and what lets Task.is_locally_owned be
        -- written as a total function rather than one that has to handle a
        -- missing local host.
        --
        -- THE ID IS GENERATED, NOT DERIVED. Nothing about the machine feeds
        -- it: not the hostname, not a MAC address, not a disk serial, not a
        -- hash of any of them. Three reasons, and each one alone is
        -- sufficient:
        --
        --   Uniqueness. Two machines can share a hostname -- two laptops
        --   imaged from the same template routinely do -- and a derived id
        --   would collide exactly where a shared board most needs them
        --   separated.
        --
        --   Stability. A hostname changes when someone renames their laptop.
        --   A derived id would change with it, and every task that machine
        --   owns would instantly look like it belonged to a machine that no
        --   longer exists. The id must outlive renames, reinstalls of the
        --   surrounding system, and the label below.
        --
        --   Disclosure. An id is the one field a shared board would compare
        --   mechanically, so it is the field most likely to end up somewhere
        --   it can be read. A hostname often carries a person's name, a team,
        --   or an asset tag. An opaque id carries nothing.
        --
        -- THE LABEL IS NOT MINTED AT ALL. This rule sets id and leaves label
        -- null, and that is deliberate rather than an omission: a label seeded
        -- here would mean the label is never unset, and a machine nobody has
        -- named would be indistinguishable from one somebody named after its
        -- hostname on purpose. The startup gate
        -- (startup.allium: PromptForHostLabelWhenUnnamed) needs exactly that
        -- distinction to know whether to ask.
        --
        -- The hostname keeps its old job and loses only its silence: it is the
        -- value the startup prompt arrives pre-filled with, so accepting it is
        -- one keypress, and it reaches the label through RenameHost below like
        -- any other answer. It is a suggestion, not an identity — the id is
        -- untouched by whatever is chosen, and no rule ever compares labels.
        --
        -- Mint is not re-run. The requires clause makes this explicit rather
        -- than relying on FirstRun firing once: an install whose Host row
        -- already exists must keep the id it has, because task rows already
        -- name it. A second mint would orphan every one of them.
        --
        -- == Black boxes ==
        -- host_file_present() asks whether a host file exists in the data
        -- directory, readable or not; host_file_path() is the data directory
        -- joined with config.host_file_name.
}

-- == Naming ==

rule RenameHost {
    when: RenameHost(label)

    requires: trim(label) != ""

    ensures:
        core/local_host().label = trim(label)
        HostRenamed()

    @guidance
        -- The label is written to the host file (IdentityLivesInHostFile),
        -- as a whole-file replacement (HostFileIsWrittenWhole).
        --
        -- Renaming changes what a human sees and nothing else. The id is
        -- deliberately absent from the ensures: it is what Task.host refers
        -- to, so changing it would orphan every task this machine owns. The
        -- two fields exist separately precisely so this operation is safe.
        --
        -- The empty string -- and whitespace-only text, which trim(label)
        -- treats the same way -- is refused because a label's whole job is
        -- to be recognisable, and a blank-looking one is worse than the
        -- hostname the prompt offered, or than the name it would replace. The
        -- stored label is the
        -- TRIMMED text, not the raw input, so leading/trailing whitespace
        -- from a pasted or padded value never becomes part of what other
        -- rules compare or display. No other validation: beyond trimming, a
        -- label is free text shown to the person who typed it.
        --
        -- THIS IS THE ONLY RULE THAT WRITES THE LABEL, and it covers both
        -- writes that exist: the first one, where the label is null and the
        -- startup gate supplies the operator's answer (startup.allium:
        -- NameHostFromStartupPrompt), and every later one. The name says
        -- "rename" and the first write is really a naming; that is accepted in
        -- exchange for having one writer rather than two, because two would be
        -- two places for the empty-label refusal and the trim to disagree.
        -- The requires clause is what makes null unreachable once set: no
        -- accepted input trims to empty, so the field goes null -> named and
        -- never back.
        --
        -- WHAT HAPPENS WHEN THE WRITE ITSELF DOES NOT TAKE is not this rule's
        -- to say, and is no longer unsaid. On the first write the caller is
        -- startup.allium's NameHostFromStartupPrompt, and a store that will not
        -- accept the label leaves the machine exactly as unnamed as it was:
        -- that caller treats the failure as its own substrate failing and
        -- stops the launch with the same named reason a failed identity
        -- read/mint gets (startup.allium: AbortWhenTheHostIdentityStoreIsUnusable
        -- names the reason; NameHostFromStartupPrompt is what actually raises
        -- it for a write, since it is the one place that attempts one), rather
        -- than drawing a board for a name nothing kept. This rule keeps
        -- describing the write it performs; it does not grow an arm for the
        -- store refusing it.
        --
        -- WHAT A RENAME MEANS BEYOND THIS INSTALL. The label is a SHARED
        -- DISPLAY VALUE, not a local preference, and this rule takes effect
        -- LOCALLY and IMMEDIATELY regardless: `core/local_host().label` is
        -- correct on this machine the instant the write commits, whether or
        -- not anyone else ever learns it. The emitted `HostRenamed()` is what
        -- gives a colleague's board a chance to learn it too — see
        -- sync.allium: RegisterHostOnRename, which mirrors the resulting row
        -- to the shared registry when a connection exists, and does nothing
        -- when one does not (the label is still correct locally; the mirror
        -- catches up on the next connection instead, via
        -- RegisterHostOnConnect). This rule stays ignorant of connections
        -- either way — see this file's Excludes for why that split holds —
        -- and nothing in it should be written as though the label were
        -- private: in particular, no rule may start branching on a label,
        -- because a value other installs can change is not one to make
        -- decisions from.
        --
        -- Two consequences of the label being shared are deliberately left
        -- open rather than decided here, and the registry existing does not
        -- settle either of them on its own. Whether two hosts may hold the
        -- same label: still unconstrained, because nothing compares labels
        -- and a uniqueness rule with a handful of rows in it has barely been
        -- tested against the case it exists for. What a stale label should
        -- look like to a reader: still undefined, because that needs a
        -- staleness clock this rule and its mirror do not add — sending a row
        -- is not the same as timestamping how old a colleague's copy of it
        -- is. See the follow-up list at the end of
        -- docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-13-distributed-dispatch-design.md.
}

-- == The person ==

rule AdoptUserIdentity {
    when: UserIdentityOffered(identity)

    requires: core/local_host().owner = null

    ensures:
        core/local_host().owner = identity
        UserIdentitySettled(identity: identity)

    @guidance
        -- The first time this install reaches a shared store, the store
        -- answers with a UserIdentity, and this is where that answer becomes
        -- permanent. From here on the install can prove it is the same person
        -- on every later connection, which is the whole point: a person who
        -- got a new identity every morning would own nothing.
        --
        -- NOTHING HERE MINTS ANYTHING. Contrast MintHostIdentity above, which
        -- generates an id locally and owes nobody an explanation. The user
        -- identity is generated by the store, arrives through the trigger, and
        -- is opaque to every rule in this system — which is exactly why
        -- core/UserIdentity is an external entity. An install that invented one
        -- would be inventing a person, and the store would not recognise them.
        --
        -- WRITTEN TO THE HOST FILE, WITH ITS CREDENTIAL. The owner and the
        -- credential the store handed out with it are written together, in
        -- one whole-file replacement (HostFileIsWrittenWhole), so the file
        -- never holds an identity without the credential that proves it or
        -- the reverse.
        --
        -- THE STORED ANSWER IS core/local_host().owner, AND THERE IS NO OTHER.
        -- "Persists across restarts" is not a separate obligation this rule
        -- discharges; it is what writing a domain field means. Had the identity
        -- been kept somewhere beside the host row, the two could disagree, and
        -- the disagreement would be invisible: a machine owned by a person the
        -- install no longer believes it is. See core/Host's
        -- LocalHostOwnerIsWrittenOnce.
        --
        -- The guard is the field being NULL, not a "first connection" flag.
        -- There is no such flag and there must not be one — a connection count
        -- and an owner field would be two records of the same fact, and the
        -- honest question is only ever "do we already know who we are?".
        --
        -- WHAT THIS DOES NOT TOUCH. Not the host id: a person acquiring a name
        -- does not change which disk holds a worktree, and every locality gate
        -- keeps comparing machine against machine (core/Task.is_locally_owned).
        -- Not the label: that is the machine's display name and is answered by
        -- a human, not by a store. Not any other Host row: a colleague's
        -- machine is owned by a colleague, and this rule cannot reach it.
}

rule ConfirmUnchangedUserIdentity {
    when: UserIdentityOffered(identity)

    requires: core/local_host().owner = identity

    ensures: UserIdentitySettled(identity: identity)

    @guidance
        -- The ordinary case, and by a very large margin the common one: every
        -- reconnect after the first, and every board start after the first.
        -- Nothing is written, because nothing changed.
        --
        -- IT IS A RULE RATHER THAN AN OMISSION BECAUSE SOMETHING DOWNSTREAM
        -- WAITS ON IT. sync.allium does not subscribe until the identity is
        -- settled, and "settled" has to be emitted on the path where it was
        -- already settled too — otherwise the first connection of a session
        -- would sync and every later one would hang, which is the kind of bug
        -- that only appears after a network blip and is then blamed on the
        -- network.
        --
        -- Emitting the same trigger as AdoptUserIdentity is deliberate. The
        -- consumer's question is "who am I, and may I proceed?", and that
        -- question has one answer whether or not this connection was the one
        -- that learned it. A separate trigger would invite a consumer to treat
        -- the two differently, and there is nothing to treat differently.
}

rule RefuseAChangedUserIdentity {
    when: UserIdentityOffered(identity)

    requires:
        core/local_host().owner != null
        and core/local_host().owner != identity

    ensures: UserIdentityConflict(stored: core/local_host().owner, offered: identity)

    @guidance
        -- The store says this install is somebody else. It is rare, it is
        -- never benign, and the two states it can leave behind are both worse
        -- than stopping.
        --
        -- HOW IT HAPPENS: the credential that proves this identity is gone or
        -- was rejected, so the store did the only thing it can do and issued a
        -- new one. From the store's side nothing is wrong. From here, a person
        -- has just been split in two.
        --
        -- WHY THE OWNER IS NOT OVERWRITTEN. Adopting the new identity is the
        -- tempting arm, and it is the destructive one: every task on the old
        -- user board (core/Task.owner) instantly belongs to a person this
        -- install is no longer, and the board that shows none of them looks
        -- exactly like a board with nothing on it. Nothing in the interface
        -- distinguishes "you have no work" from "your work is now somebody
        -- else's", and by the time anyone notices, the new identity has
        -- written rows of its own.
        --
        -- WHY IT IS FATAL RATHER THAN A WARNING. The remedy is a choice only a
        -- human can make — restore the credential, or accept a new identity on
        -- purpose — and neither is a thing the board can do on their behalf. A
        -- warning on a board that keeps working is a warning that gets dismissed
        -- once and never seen again. What "fatal" means concretely, on a cold
        -- start and on a mid-session reconnect alike, is sync.allium's
        -- (StopOnAUserIdentityConflict); this rule refuses the write and names
        -- what it saw.
        --
        -- The trigger carries BOTH identities because a person debugging this
        -- needs to know which one their tasks are under, and the one the board
        -- no longer holds is the one they cannot look up afterwards.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

-- This spec declares no surface of its own, and that is a decision rather than
-- a gap. There used to be a HostLabelEditor here — a "rename this machine"
-- editor facing the user — and nothing in the system ever reached it: no key,
-- no command and no MCP tool produced its trigger. A specified surface nobody
-- can open is the same defect as a label seeded where nobody can see it, so it
-- was removed rather than left standing as a description of something that
-- does not exist.
--
-- The only surface that sets a label today is the mandatory startup prompt, and
-- it lives in startup.allium (HostLabelPrompt) because what it really governs
-- is whether the board may draw. RenameHost above stays: it is the write, it is
-- reachable from that prompt, and it is ready for a genuine "rename later"
-- surface. Adding one is future work, and it belongs here — in this file,
-- beside the rule it drives — the moment something in the TUI or CLI can
-- actually open it.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------


